/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Large Binocular Telescope Observatory)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Intro to the topic:  A new development in the field of adaptive optics \(AO\) on ground-based telescopes enables routine monitoring of changes on Jupiter's volcanic moon Io at scales down to ~80km \(achieved\), or even down to ~50km \(in the limit\). Our observations of Io, taken with SHARK-VIS on the Large Binocular Telescope \(LBT\) in Arizona, demonstrate this new capability. SHARK-VIS, which saw first light at LBT on October 2nd, 2023, is one of only a few visible-light AO instruments on large telescopes.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Summary:)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (One of the key instruments, Shark-Viz, uses just one of the LBT's two 8.4-meter mirrors, but the next step is to combine the light from both mirrors \(8.4 meters each\) to create a 23-meter effective baseline. This combination is achieved through the LBT Interferometer \(LBTI\), which allows for high-resolution imaging at much higher detail. For instance, observing at 5-micron wavelengths, LBT can resolve surface features of moons like Io \(one of Jupiter's moons\) with a resolution of 20-30 km, far surpassing current spacecraft observations in resolution.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The potential for further resolution improvements lies in the new LBT interferometer visible extension \(LIVE\), which would provide three times better resolution, pushing down to 5 milliarcseconds. This could result in surface resolution on objects like Io reaching as small as 5-10 kilometers, a level comparable to spacecraft observations, but from the ground. The speaker suggests this will mark a new era in planetary imaging from Earth-based telescopes.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Additionally, the conversation touches on the innovative use of deformable mirrors in adaptive optics, allowing for real-time corrections to atmospheric distortions, significantly enhancing the image clarity. This technology involves thin, flexible mirrors with actuators that adjust to correct distortions, a major leap from earlier thick mirrors that could not be adjusted as easily.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Overall, the LBT, with its advanced instruments, promises to push the boundaries of ground-based astronomical observations, offering an unprecedented level of detail for both planetary and extragalactic science.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Gifting for Astronomers.  A few tips by Stu Brackney)
/E (Gifting for Astronomers.  A few tips by Stu Brackney)
/Alt ()
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
